  Since reminders are a confirmed next step, a due date isn't speculative — it's a requirement we already know about, so
  it belongs in the schema now. Everything beyond that is a judgment call about how much app you want.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · How much should a task carry? → Title + done + due date (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Last question before I present the design.

  This is a new project, so it's worth deciding tooling now — it's never cheaper than before the first line of code
  exists. One tension to name honestly: we just chose "no dependencies," and test/lint tooling means a [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m with
  devDependencies. Those are different things — the [3mshipped[0m app stays zero-dependency and buildless either way; the
  tooling is dev-time only and a user with a browser never sees it. But it is still a [38;5;153mnode_modules[39m directory in the
  repo, so it's your call whether that's worth it.

  The logic worth testing here is real and pure: "is this task overdue given a clock," and reading/writing the storage
  format including old records. That's exactly the kind of code where tests pay for themselves, and exactly the kind
  that breaks silently when it doesn't have them.

